00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
now on monday the eyes of the world will  return to minneapolis  the scene of george floyd's killing the  city is braced for the trial of derek  chauvin a former police officer accused  of murdering mr floyd  the trial's being hailed as one of the  most important in u.s history and a  defining moment  for the nation and the civil rights  movement and joining me now to discuss  this is martin luther king  the third civil rights activist and the  son of martin luther king jr thank you  so much for joining us  um we'll get to george floyd and the  court case in a moment but i just wanted  to talk to you about um  a new voting law in georgia among other  things that imposes new id  requirements for mail-in voting limits  the availability of ballot drop boxes  a host of other things a 98-page measure  um it's been likened to the racist  policies of  the of um the deep south  um said that it's this the president  excuse me says it disproportionately  targets black americans  what do you make of this new law well  there's no question about that  the law was signed by the governor i  believe on thursday  and immediately a number of  community organizations came together to  file a lawsuit  there's even a small provision or a  provision in this law that says  if you bring water to someone who is  standing in line who may have been  standing in line for four or five hours  it is a crime it is the most inhumane  kind of thing that i've ever seen and  the only reason that it exists  is because um you know  on january 5th the state of georgia  elected its first black senator and its  first  jewish senator john ossoff reverend  murphy on walnut  uh who's the pastor of ebenezer church  where my father was co-pastor  um and and the the second issue of  course in november of last year  we had an election and for the first  time the state of georgia elected  uh the voted for the democratic ticket  uh president biden and vice president  harris  so clearly this provision is only  to stifle and slow down black  people students browns people  and others from voting it really makes  no sense in fact the governor  and the secretary of state said we had  no issues with the election in november  nor the election on january 5th so why  they would come and create these  draconian law  laws is very suspect and the fact  that they do hearken back to jim crow  the jim crow era  where blacks were not allowed to  register because they'd ask questions  like  well how high is up or how many bottle  bubbles are in a bottle  of soap who knows those answers um so  jim crow just to give  some sort of context there were laws  that enforced racial segregation and  they were actually enforced until the  mid  60s um donald trump  he lost georgia by about 12 000 votes  last year he has celebrated these  changes you mentioned the governor brian  kemp he said  there's nothing jim crow about requiring  a photo or state issued id to vote by  absentee ballot  every georgia voter must already do so  when voting in person  president biden the left and the  national media are determined to destroy  the sanctity and security of the ballot  box what do you say to that  well first of all when you talk about  reducing  polling hours and play places for  example we used to have maybe a month  and a half  to vote early they cut that back  maybe to less than 30 days there are  were certain boxes  where you could go and drop off your  ballots  now you have to have some kind of  notification or notarized uh  scenario uh to make sure your ballot is  legal it's all it  and again some of these things maybe if  there was any proof of fraud of some  kind  in fact there was no fraud at all the  only thing that happened  is more people voted than ever before  and they want to restrict people  it's interesting that we call ourselves  a democracy  and we operate around the world talking  about  uh imposing or encouraging other  countries to engage in democracy  but yet right at home we are suppressing  democracy  it's not about an id that's not the  issue  the fact of the matter is in black  communities in brown communities  there are lines sometimes that take  hours to vote  and because of that you should open up  more lines or add more days for voting  there are provisions that you do to  expand the right to vote  not to subtract and reduce and what  these people have done  is they have created a scenario to  subtract  by the way just as they signed the law  in the governor's office and one of the  state representatives  was arrested for knocking on the door  when public business was being done  and other legislators were in there but  they did not want her in there  and so they arrested a sitting state  representative  who was trying to be a part of the  process because she represents georgians  also  that's just unconscionable what this  state has done it's a sad  sad time for the state of georgia and  i think for the united states because  there are 40 more states  that are also looking at legislation  in my reading about this i came across  um deborah scott the executive director  of georgia stand up  and she said we're resilient we have  survived slavery we have survived the  first  jim crow and will definitely survive jim  crow in a siesta suit in 2021  one wonders when black  americans don't have to be resilient  resilient enough to survive something as  appalling as slavery  when they can just go about their  business and exercise their democracy  well you know that's a very good  question and observation  and the fact of the matter is the again  these draconian laws  should not be uh enforced  uh or should not be passed around this  country  um but they are being passed and that  just means  that we do have to and we will be  vigilant by the way we're going to  always  be vigilant in fact my mom used to say  that every generation has to  re has to earn its its keep or its  freedom  so freedom is just not a permanent thing  we would hope that there would be a day  where that would happen  as you may remember my father in his  last speech  talked about getting to the promised  land and he said i've  been to the mountaintop i've seen the  promised land  i may not get there with your but i know  that we as a people  will get to the promised land and that  meant all people not just black people  but all people  who are suffering uh in our nation and  really around the world  and so again we continue to be vigilant  and ultimately we will one day address  these issues  in a way and new governing people will  be put in place  so that all of the people are  represented  truly with fairness and equity i'm glad  you brought up your parents  um coretta scott king martin luther king  jr  um you are the oldest living child just  to give people who don't know your  context  with everything that your father went  through with everything your mother went  through with everything you've been  through  how do you feel as an american today  i i certainly feel very saddened about  where we are  but i'm also very hopeful because there  are young people all over the world  protesting for right um  and and so that's that's that is  certainly inspiring  yeah i have a we my wife and i have a 12  year old daughter  uh and she's an activist a social  justice activist but there are many  young people  her age who are you know when i was her  age i didn't know  anything about the issues that were  going on around the world  but she knows about the issues she knows  about gun violence  she knows about these voting issues  and she and her friends are standing up  with  others across this nation the parkland  students they  are protesting in a positive way in fact  tragically after george floyd and i know  where  the child is going on right now  tragically after his death  there were civil rights demonstrations  in every state in our nation  the largest we've ever seen many of  those led by white  young people but it was whites it was  blacks it was  uh latinos hispanics it was asians it  was uh  wealthy people it was poor people so all  coming together and of course  protests obviously taking place in  europe taking place in australia  taking place in south america uh on the  african continent just about every  continent  and he's thinking i'm i'm hopeful  please forgive me for jumping in there  but i really want to know what would you  tell  george floyd if you could speak to  george floyd what would you tell him  well first of all what i hope  and and speaking to george floyd if i  could speak to george floyd we wouldn't  be at this moment  uh we are a pivotal moment in the  history of our nation  oftentimes when policemen are charged  they are very rarely if ever convicted  my hope is that a conviction occurs but  the sad part about it  is this man should not have had to lose  his life  for you know it doesn't matter in this  context because  but it was certainly a small crime but  the fact of the matter is he was asking  for help  and this policeman stood on his neck and  we saw the breath taken  away from him and that should not have  happened  that should never happen again  policemen have to be held accountable  and  if justice uh with a conviction  uh is not seen that's the thing that i  worry about uh and obviously the city of  minneapolis  we're gonna have to leave it there i'm  so sorry we're gonna have to leave it  there thank you so much for talking to  us  martin luther king iii thank you so much  thank you

